import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse('{"title":"lib-lang — Expose the Handl language as a reusable library","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"lib-lang — Expose the Handl language as a reusable library","type":"adr","date":"2026-08-24T00:00:00.000Z","status":"superseded","status_note":"This ADR proposed a monolithic `ac-lib-lang` façade over a trait-based AST\\nand Rust emitters. None of that survives ADR-0044 (Language as Data and\\nRuntime Split), which replaced the trait-based AST with a serializable,\\nstring-keyed `Ast` arena in `handl-language` and split the runtime into\\nregistry-based engine selection. ADR-0047 is the honest replacement: the\\n`lib-lang` surface is now a set of granular `plugin-lib-lang-*` leaf\\ncrates (`lex`/`parse`/`eval`/`compile`/`run_compiled`), each depending on\\n`handl-runtime`\'s `RuntimeFactory`/`RuntimeHandle` traits — not a single\\nfaçade crate. See ADR-0047\'s \\"ADR-0030 superseded\\" note for the full\\nrationale.","superseded_by":["ADR-0047"]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/adrs/0030-lib-lang.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/adrs/0030-lib-lang.md"}'),a={name:`handl/project/adrs/0030-lib-lang.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="adr-0030-lib-lang-—-expose-the-handl-language-as-a-reusable-library" tabindex="-1">ADR 0030: lib-lang — Expose the Handl language as a reusable library <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0030-lib-lang-—-expose-the-handl-language-as-a-reusable-library" aria-label="Permalink to “ADR 0030: lib-lang — Expose the Handl language as a reusable library”">​</a></h1><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><p>Handl (fka Astral Chronicle / ac-lang) is currently packaged as a monolithic compiler and runtime. The lexer, parser, AST, typechecker, and compiler passes are not exposed as a single, stable library. This makes it impossible for a Handl program to parse, inspect, or evaluate Handl code from within Handl itself.</p><p>Use cases that motivate a <code>lib-lang</code> façade:</p><ol><li><strong>Handl inside Handl</strong>: A Handl program should be able to instantiate a sandboxed Handl VM, choose which modules to expose, and evaluate a Handl expression or a small Handl source inside that sandbox.</li><li><strong>Tooling</strong>: Formatters, LSPs, documentation generators, and test runners should reuse the same lexer, parser, and AST without depending on the full compiler or runtime workspace.</li><li><strong>Higher-level libraries</strong>: <code>lib-template</code> (or <code>lib-escaping</code>) should not own expression parsing or evaluation. They should call into a public language API.</li></ol><p>The question is what the public surface of that library should be, and how it relates to the existing compiler/runtime crates.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><p>Introduce a top-level library <code>lib-lang</code> (crate <code>ac-lib-lang</code> under <code>crates/lib-lang</code>). This library is the curated, public façade for everything needed to parse, inspect, compile, and evaluate Handl source. It re-exports stable APIs from the existing compiler and VM crates and hides unstable internals.</p><p><code>lib-lang</code> is organized into submodules that mirror the language pipeline:</p><ul><li><code>lang.lexer</code> — tokenization and <code>LexerConfig</code> composition.</li><li><code>lang.parser</code> — expression and statement parsing into the canonical AST.</li><li><code>lang.ast</code> — the canonical AST and traversal utilities.</li><li><code>lang.typecheck</code> — type inference and schema validation, where stable.</li><li><code>lang.compiler</code> — lowering to the canonical VM format.</li><li><code>lang.vm</code> — a configurable, sandboxed VM instance.</li></ul><h3 id="lang-vm-and-sandboxing" tabindex="-1"><code>lang.vm</code> and sandboxing <a class="header-anchor" href="#lang-vm-and-sandboxing" aria-label="Permalink to “lang.vm and sandboxing”">​</a></h3><p>A Handl program can instantiate a child Handl VM through <code>lang.vm.sandbox</code>:</p><div class="language-"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>let sandbox = lang.vm.sandbox({</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  modules: [&quot;math&quot;, &quot;str&quot;],</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  timeout: 1000,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>})</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>let result = lang.vm.eval(sandbox, &quot;add(1, 2)&quot;)</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The sandbox is a full <code>Vm</code> with a user-composed <code>ModuleHost</code>. It does not share context with the host VM unless explicitly passed. This is the supported mechanism for &quot;Handl inside Handl&quot;, including template evaluation and user-defined functions.</p><h3 id="boundaries" tabindex="-1">Boundaries <a class="header-anchor" href="#boundaries" aria-label="Permalink to “Boundaries”">​</a></h3><p><code>lib-lang</code> does <strong>not</strong> include:</p><ul><li><code>lib-template</code> — template parsing and rendering is a consumer of <code>lib-lang</code> and <code>lib-escaping</code>. It lives in its own crate and depends on <code>lib-lang</code> only for parsing and sandboxed evaluation.</li><li><code>lib-escaping</code> — context-specific escaping (HTML, URL, CSS, JS, etc.) is a separate abstraction that <code>lib-template</code>, <code>lib-http</code>, and other output layers can share. It lives in its own crate.</li><li><code>lib-utils</code> — generic pure utility functions (math, string transforms, predicates, etc.) remain in <code>lib-utils</code>. They may be exposed to a sandbox VM through the <code>lib-utils</code> module, but they are not part of <code>lib-lang</code>.</li></ul><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><h3 id="positive" tabindex="-1">Positive <a class="header-anchor" href="#positive" aria-label="Permalink to “Positive”">​</a></h3><ul><li>Handl can reason about and evaluate Handl code from within Handl.</li><li>Tooling can depend on a narrow <code>lib-lang</code> façade instead of the entire compiler workspace.</li><li>The template engine and escaping concerns can be decoupled from the language implementation.</li><li>Sandboxed execution has a single, well-defined entry point (<code>lang.vm.sandbox</code>).</li></ul><h3 id="trade-offs" tabindex="-1">Trade-offs <a class="header-anchor" href="#trade-offs" aria-label="Permalink to “Trade-offs”">​</a></h3><ul><li><code>lib-lang</code> becomes a large façade. We must be disciplined about what is public and what is internal to avoid leaking unstable compiler internals.</li><li>Maintaining a stable <code>lib-lang</code> API may slow compiler refactors. Internals should remain in <code>ac-compiler-*</code> / <code>ac-ast-*</code> crates; only curated, versioned APIs re-export through <code>lib-lang</code>.</li><li>The <code>lang.vm.sandbox</code> API needs careful design to prevent confusion between host scope and sandbox scope.</li></ul><h2 id="open-questions-future-adrs" tabindex="-1">Open questions / future ADRs <a class="header-anchor" href="#open-questions-future-adrs" aria-label="Permalink to “Open questions / future ADRs”">​</a></h2><ul><li>The exact domain and public API of <code>lib-template</code> is recorded in <code>adrs/0031-lib-template-and-escaping.md</code>.</li><li>The escaping interface and where it lives is a dedicated <code>lib-escaping</code> ADR (proposed).</li><li>Whether <code>lib-lang</code> should expose the canonical expression evaluator or a separate sandbox-specific evaluator.</li></ul><h2 id="references" tabindex="-1">References <a class="header-anchor" href="#references" aria-label="Permalink to “References”">​</a></h2><ul><li><code>adrs/0001-lexer-decoupling.md</code></li><li><code>adrs/0002-lego-vm-composition.md</code></li><li><code>adrs/0004-colorless-execution.md</code></li><li><code>adrs/0031-lib-template-and-escaping.md</code></li></ul>`,26)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};